# Line endings are load-bearing in this repo, not a style preference.
#
# Almost every claim this project makes is a SHA-256 taken over file bytes:
# seed/CHECKSUMS.txt, corpus/CHECKSUMS.txt, the sha256 column in corpus/PARSE.csv,
# the prompt fingerprint in evals/PROMPT_FREEZE.json, and the seed_fingerprint on
# every expected result set in evals/sql/questions.jsonl. A CRLF checkout changes
# every one of those bytes, so the hashes stop matching while the content is
# identical.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, and until 2026-08-11 there was
# nothing here to override it. A fresh Windows clone therefore failed two tests
# with messages that accused the wrong things — "prompt changed since the freeze"
# and "49 expectations were computed against a different seed". Neither had
# changed; LF-normalising the bytes reproduced both recorded hashes exactly.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the index AND checks out LF on every
# platform, which is what makes the hashes portable. Do not relax it to `eol=`
# or remove it: the failure is silent on Linux and misattributed on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# CSVs are written by a writer that fixes its own line terminator, so git must
# not have an opinion about them. `-text` passes the bytes through verbatim in
# both directions.
#
# This rule is not defensive tidiness — the blanket rule above breaks the build
# without it. Python's csv module defaults to lineterminator="\r\n" (RFC 4180),
# and corpus/MANIFEST.csv and corpus/PARSE.csv are committed with CRLF because
# nothing overrides it. corpus/CHECKSUMS.txt records the CRLF hash of
# MANIFEST.csv, so normalising it to LF changes the bytes and fails
# verify-corpus — the exact check this file exists to keep working. Caught by
# running `git add --renormalize .` before committing this file, which is worth
# doing again if these rules ever change.
#
# seed/*.csv are LF, because api/scripts/seed.py passes lineterminator="\n"
# explicitly. The two conventions disagree and the corpus side is the odd one
# out; reconciling it rewrites MANIFEST.csv and therefore CHECKSUMS.txt, so it
# is a deliberate regeneration, not a cleanup. `-text` holds both as committed.
*.csv   -text

# Binary files must never be newline-translated or diffed as text. The corpus
# PDFs are checksummed in corpus/CHECKSUMS.txt, so a translated byte here would
# fail verify-corpus rather than pass quietly — but it would fail confusingly.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.onnx  binary
